  <article aria-labelledby="drevan-heading">
    <h3 id="drevan-heading">The World of Drevan</h3>

    <p>
      Long ago, Drevan was home to civilizations whose mastery
      of magic and technology reshaped the world. Great machines
      drew power from mysterious energies, ancient structures
      stretched toward the heavens, and the boundaries between
      science and sorcery all but disappeared.
    </p>

    <p>Then, something changed.</p>

    <p>
      The great civilizations fell, leaving behind scattered
      settlements, forgotten machines, and ruins filled with
      secrets. Much of Drevan's history has been lost, and
      few agree on what brought the old world to an end.
    </p>

    <p>
      Yet life continues. Adventurers seek fortune among the
      remnants of the past, warriors test their strength,
      and merchants trade in everything from ordinary supplies
      to strange artifacts of unknown origin.
    </p>

    <h4>Nexaris</h4>

    <p>
      Among the scattered settlements of Drevan lies Nexaris,
      a refuge for wanderers, fighters, merchants, and those
      seeking a new beginning.
    </p>

    <p>
      Neither a great city nor a simple village, Nexaris is
      a gathering place built upon the remains of the old
      world. Here, ancient machinery stands alongside humble
      dwellings, and the extraordinary has become part of
      everyday life.
    </p>

    <p>
      Within its grounds, warriors enter the Dominion to
      earn gold and glory, travelers seek healing at the
      Sanctum, and merchants offer their wares at the
      Curious Emporium. Others spend their days fishing,
      gathering supplies, or preparing for whatever
      challenges await.
    </p>

    <p>
      There are no kings to serve, no prophecies to fulfill,
      and no single path to follow. In Nexaris, each
      traveler is free to forge their own destiny.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>The old world is gone. The unknown awaits.</em>
    </p>
  </article>

  <article aria-labelledby="absolute-heading">
    <h3 id="absolute-heading">The Absolute</h3>

    <p><em>The Last Truth</em></p>

    <h4>The Age Before</h4>

    <p>
      Long before the founding of Nexaris, before the great
      civilizations of Drevan were reduced to dust and memory,
      the old world stood at the height of its power.
    </p>

    <p>
      Magic and technology had become indistinguishable.
      Machines bent the laws of nature, ancient scholars
      harnessed the energies of distant dimensions, and
      entire cities flourished beneath artificial stars.
    </p>

    <p>
      Yet for all their achievements, the scholars of the
      old world remained haunted by a single question.
    </p>

    <p><em>What existed before creation?</em></p>

    <h4>The Axiom Engine</h4>

    <p>
      Deep beneath the surface of Drevan, the greatest minds
      of the age constructed a machine unlike anything the
      world had ever known.
    </p>

    <p>They called it the Axiom Engine.</p>

    <p>
      Its purpose was not to create, destroy, or conquer.
      It was designed to reach beyond the boundaries of
      existence itself, to uncover the fundamental truth
      upon which all reality was built.
    </p>

    <p>
      For centuries, the machine remained silent.
      Generations of scholars devoted their lives to
      its operation, searching for a response from
      whatever might exist beyond the known universe.
    </p>

    <p>Then, one day, the machine answered.</p>

    <h4>The Last Truth</h4>

    <p>
      What the scholars discovered was not a distant
      dimension, an ancient god, or an inexhaustible
      source of power.
    </p>

    <p>
      They had reached something that existed outside
      the boundaries of reality. Something that had
      neither beginning nor end.
    </p>

    <p>
      It could not be measured. It could not be understood.
      And it could not be contained.
    </p>

    <p>
      The entity did not regard the universe with hatred
      or malice. Such concepts were beneath its understanding.
    </p>

    <p>To it, existence was not a miracle.</p>

    <p><strong>Existence was an error.</strong></p>

    <p>
      The scholars gave this impossible intelligence
      a name.
    </p>

    <p><strong>The Absolute.</strong></p>

    <h4>The Great Unmaking</h4>

    <p>
      Realizing the danger of their discovery, the
      scholars attempted to sever the connection
      between the Axiom Engine and the entity beyond.
    </p>

    <p>But the connection had already changed the world.</p>

    <p>
      The laws of existence began to unravel.
      Cities vanished without destruction.
      Entire populations disappeared from history,
      leaving behind monuments to people who had
      never existed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Machines continued their endless labor for
      masters whose names had been erased from
      memory. Roads led to cities that had never
      been built. Ancient records contradicted
      the memories of those who had written them.
    </p>

    <p>Reality itself was being rewritten.</p>

    <p>
      The collapse spread across Drevan, consuming
      the achievements of countless generations.
      No army could oppose it. No weapon could
      strike it. No spell could restore what
      had been unmade.
    </p>

    <h4>The Final Seal</h4>

    <p>
      In desperation, the surviving scholars
      gathered at the Axiom Engine for one
      final undertaking.
    </p>

    <p>
      Unable to destroy the machine or the
      intelligence connected to it, they
      constructed an immense network of
      magical barriers and mechanical seals.
    </p>

    <p>
      Layer upon layer of stone, metal, and
      ancient machinery was placed between
      the surface world and the terrible
      truth beneath it.
    </p>

    <p>
      The surviving records offer no account
      of what became of the scholars.
      Whether they perished, escaped, or
      were themselves erased from existence
      remains unknown.
    </p>

    <p>But the unmaking stopped.</p>

    <p>
      The world endured, broken and forever
      changed.
    </p>

    <h4>The Ruins</h4>

    <p>
      Thousands of years passed.
      New settlements rose among the remnants
      of the old world. Forgotten machines
      became relics. Ancient sciences became
      indistinguishable from magic.
    </p>

    <p>The truth of the catastrophe faded into legend.</p>

    <p>
      The enormous structures surrounding
      the buried Axiom Engine became known
      simply as The Ruins.
    </p>

    <p>
      Explorers ventured into their upper
      chambers in search of lost treasures,
      ancient technology, and the secrets
      of civilizations long forgotten.
    </p>

    <p>
      Few returned from the deeper levels.
      Those who did spoke of impossible
      architecture, machines that operated
      without power, and voices whispering
      in languages no living scholar
      could identify.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some claimed the deeper chambers
      were not ruins at all, but the
      remains of a prison.
    </p>

    <h4>What Remains</h4>

    <p>
      Beneath the deepest chambers,
      beyond the reach of even the
      most determined explorers,
      the Axiom Engine continues
      its ancient operation.
    </p>

    <p>
      Its machinery has not fallen silent.
      Its connection has not been severed.
    </p>

    <p>
      And whatever waits beyond that
      connection has never abandoned
      its purpose.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some scholars believe The Absolute
      destroyed the old world.
    </p>

    <p>
      Others suggest the entity was
      merely attempting to correct
      something humanity was never
      meant to understand.
    </p>

    <p>
      A few surviving fragments of
      ancient writing suggest an even
      more troubling possibility:
      that the scholars misunderstood
      what they had discovered, and
      that The Absolute was not the
      greatest danger beyond creation.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>